A revoke that recurses into each child
takes a frame per level of the derivation tree,
and a deep enough tree overflows the kernel's stack.
It revokes the same nodes and says what pays for its loop,
so only the stack check catches it.

Caught-by: stack-depth

diff --git a/kernel/cap.c b/kernel/cap.c
index c22b037..0bfc66f 100644
--- a/kernel/cap.c
+++ b/kernel/cap.c
@@ -200,7 +200,10 @@ bool cap_revoke_below(struct cap *root, bool preempt)
 
 void cap_revoke(struct cap *n)
 {
-    cap_revoke_below(n, false);
+    while (n->child != 0) {
+        LOOP_PAID(cap_revoke, node, "a child it revokes");
+        cap_revoke(node(n->child));
+    }
     if (n->next != LINK_UP) {
         detach(n, false);
     }
